### What is the difference between @RestController and @Controller?

`@Controller` is a stereotype annotation that marks a class as a web controller. Its handler methods return a view name (e.g., `"account-details"`) that the view resolver maps to an HTML template.

`@RestController` combines `@Controller` and `@ResponseBody`. Every handler method returns an object that Spring serializes to JSON (via Jackson) and writes directly to the HTTP response body.

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Controller;
import org.springframework.ui.Model;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PathVariable;

// @Controller -- returns a VIEW name
@Controller
public class AccountPageController {

    @GetMapping("/pages/accounts/{id}")
    public String accountPage(@PathVariable String id, Model model) {
        model.addAttribute("accountId", id);
        return "account-details"; // resolves to templates/account-details.html
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.service.AccountService;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

// @RestController -- returns JSON directly
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/accounts")
public class AccountApiController {
    private final AccountService accountService;

    public AccountApiController(AccountService accountService) {
        this.accountService = accountService;
    }

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public Account getAccount(@PathVariable String id) {
        return accountService.findById(id); // serialized to JSON automatically
    }
}
```

```text
GET /api/accounts/SAV2001
Response:
{
  "id": "SAV2001",
  "holderName": "Priya Sharma",
  "balance": 50000.00,
  "type": "SAVINGS"
}
```

### How do @RequestMapping shortcuts work?

`@RequestMapping` is the base annotation that maps requests by URL pattern and HTTP method. The shortcut annotations specify the method and keep the code cleaner.

| Shortcut | Equivalent |
|---|---|
| `@GetMapping("/accounts")` | `@RequestMapping(value = "/accounts", method = GET)` |
| `@PostMapping("/accounts")` | `@RequestMapping(value = "/accounts", method = POST)` |
| `@PutMapping("/accounts/{id}")` | `@RequestMapping(value = "/accounts/{id}", method = PUT)` |
| `@DeleteMapping("/accounts/{id}")` | `@RequestMapping(value = "/accounts/{id}", method = DELETE)` |
| `@PatchMapping("/accounts/{id}")` | `@RequestMapping(value = "/accounts/{id}", method = PATCH)` |

`@RequestMapping` at the class level sets the base path. Method-level annotations append to it:

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.dto.CreateAccountRequest;
import com.bank.service.AccountService;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import jakarta.validation.Valid;
import java.util.List;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/accounts") // base path
public class AccountController {
    private final AccountService accountService;

    public AccountController(AccountService accountService) {
        this.accountService = accountService;
    }

    @GetMapping                    // GET /api/accounts
    public List<Account> list() {
        return accountService.findAll();
    }

    @GetMapping("/{id}")          // GET /api/accounts/SAV2001
    public Account get(@PathVariable String id) {
        return accountService.findById(id);
    }

    @PostMapping                   // POST /api/accounts
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.CREATED)
    public Account create(@Valid @RequestBody CreateAccountRequest req) {
        return accountService.create(req);
    }

    @PutMapping("/{id}")          // PUT /api/accounts/SAV2001
    public Account update(@PathVariable String id,
                          @Valid @RequestBody CreateAccountRequest req) {
        return accountService.update(id, req);
    }

    @DeleteMapping("/{id}")       // DELETE /api/accounts/SAV2001
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.NO_CONTENT)
    public void delete(@PathVariable String id) {
        accountService.delete(id);
    }
}
```

### How is @PathVariable different from @RequestParam?

`@PathVariable` extracts a value from the URL path. Use it for resource identification (the thing you want).

`@RequestParam` extracts a value from the query string. Use it for filtering, sorting, pagination, and optional parameters.

| Aspect | @PathVariable | @RequestParam |
|---|---|---|
| Source | URL path: `/accounts/{id}` | Query string: `?status=active` |
| Purpose | Resource identification | Filtering, pagination, options |
| Required | Yes by default | Yes by default (`required=false` to opt out) |
| Default value | Not supported | `defaultValue = "0"` |
| Multiple | `/accounts/{id}/transactions/{txId}` | `?page=0&size=20&sort=date` |

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import com.bank.model.Transaction;
import com.bank.service.TransactionService;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/accounts")
public class TransactionController {
    private final TransactionService txService;

    public TransactionController(TransactionService txService) {
        this.txService = txService;
    }

    // @PathVariable for resource identification
    // @RequestParam for filtering and pagination
    @GetMapping("/{accountId}/transactions")
    public List<Transaction> getTransactions(
            @PathVariable String accountId,
            @RequestParam(required = false) String type,
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "0") BigDecimal minAmount,
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "0") int page,
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "20") int size) {
        return txService.find(accountId, type, minAmount, page, size);
    }
}
```

```text
GET /api/accounts/SAV2001/transactions?type=DEBIT&minAmount=5000&page=0&size=10

  accountId = "SAV2001"     (from URL path)
  type      = "DEBIT"       (from query string)
  minAmount = 5000          (from query string)
  page      = 0             (from query string)
  size      = 10            (from query string)
```

### How does @RequestBody work internally?

When a request arrives with `Content-Type: application/json`, Spring's `DispatcherServlet` delegates to `HandlerAdapter`, which uses `HandlerMethodArgumentResolver` to resolve method parameters. For `@RequestBody`, `RequestResponseBodyMethodProcessor` calls Jackson's `MappingJackson2HttpMessageConverter` to read the input stream and deserialize the JSON bytes into the target Java class.

If the JSON is malformed, Jackson throws `HttpMessageNotReadableException` (HTTP 400). If `@Valid` is present, Bean Validation runs after deserialization and throws `MethodArgumentNotValidException` (HTTP 400) for constraint violations.

```java
package com.bank.dto;

import jakarta.validation.constraints.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public record CreateAccountRequest(
    @NotBlank(message = "Holder name is required")
    String holderName,

    @NotNull(message = "Initial deposit is required")
    @DecimalMin(value = "1000", message = "Minimum deposit is 1000")
    BigDecimal initialDeposit,

    @NotBlank(message = "Account type is required")
    @Pattern(regexp = "SAVINGS|CURRENT", message = "Type must be SAVINGS or CURRENT")
    String accountType
) {}
```

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import com.bank.dto.CreateAccountRequest;
import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.service.AccountService;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import jakarta.validation.Valid;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/accounts")
public class AccountController {
    private final AccountService accountService;

    public AccountController(AccountService accountService) {
        this.accountService = accountService;
    }

    @PostMapping
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.CREATED)
    public Account create(@Valid @RequestBody CreateAccountRequest req) {
        return accountService.create(req);
    }
}
```

